Rather than deciding whether the value should move up or down, this version rebuilds the min-heap after changing the requested position. It runs sift-down from the last internal node to the root. This is less efficient than repairing one path, but the logic is easy to check and works for either increase or decrease.

The array is modified in-place. An empty array or an invalid index is ignored.

```python
class Solution:
    def heapify(self, nums, ind, val):
        if not nums or ind < 0 or ind >= len(nums):
            return

        nums[ind] = val
        last_parent = (len(nums) - 2) // 2

        for index in range(last_parent, -1, -1):
            self._sift_down(nums, index)

    def _sift_down(self, nums, index):
        n = len(nums)

        while True:
            left = 2 * index + 1
            right = 2 * index + 2
            smallest = index

            if left < n and nums[left] < nums[smallest]:
                smallest = left
            if right < n and nums[right] < nums[smallest]:
                smallest = right

            if smallest == index:
                return

            nums[index], nums[smallest] = nums[smallest], nums[index]
            index = smallest
```

For a one-element heap, `last_parent` is `-1`, so no sift-down is needed after the assignment.

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case for rebuilding the heap.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

This is a slower but simple version. After setting `nums[ind]`, it scans every parent-child pair and swaps whenever a child is smaller than its parent. The scan repeats because a swap can move a value upward and create a new problem with its own parent.

It does not need separate upward and downward functions. The trade-off is speed: it may scan the whole array many times instead of following just one path.

A heap with duplicate values is fine because equal parent and child values do not need a swap.

```java
class Solution {
    public void heapify(int[] nums, int ind, int val) {
        if (nums == null || ind < 0 || ind >= nums.length) {
            return;
        }

        nums[ind] = val;
        boolean changed = true;

        while (changed) {
            changed = false;

            for (int child = 1; child < nums.length; child++) {
                int parent = (child - 1) / 2;
                if (nums[child] < nums[parent]) {
                    int temp = nums[child];
                    nums[child] = nums[parent];
                    nums[parent] = temp;
                    changed = true;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case, since several full scans may be needed.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The changed value only affects the path from `ind` to the root or to the leaves. If the new value is smaller, it can break the parent rule, so it moves upward. Otherwise, it may be larger than a child, so it moves downward.

The array is changed in-place. For example, with `[1, 3, 5, 7, 9]`, changing index `1` to `0` gives `[1, 0, 5, 7, 9]`. The value moves above `1`, giving `[0, 1, 5, 7, 9]`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void heapify(vector<int> &nums, int ind, int val) {
        if (nums.empty() || ind < 0 || ind >= nums.size()) {
            return;
        }

        int oldValue = nums[ind];
        nums[ind] = val;

        if (val < oldValue) {
            while (ind > 0) {
                int parent = (ind - 1) / 2;
                if (nums[parent] <= nums[ind]) {
                    break;
                }
                swap(nums[parent], nums[ind]);
                ind = parent;
            }
        } else if (val > oldValue) {
            int n = nums.size();
            while (true) {
                int left = 2 * ind + 1;
                int right = 2 * ind + 2;
                int smallest = ind;

                if (left < n && nums[left] < nums[smallest]) {
                    smallest = left;
                }
                if (right < n && nums[right] < nums[smallest]) {
                    smallest = right;
                }

                if (smallest == ind) {
                    break;
                }

                swap(nums[ind], nums[smallest]);
                ind = smallest;
            }
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)` because the value travels along one heap path.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

This version restores the heap with recursion. After assigning the new value, it first checks the parent. If the parent is too large, the value moves up and the same check continues. If the parent is fine, the smaller child is checked instead.

A leaf node has no children, so the downward call simply stops there. The input array is modified directly.

Dry run: for `[1, 4, 3, 7, 8]`, changing index `1` to `6` gives `[1, 6, 3, 7, 8]`. The smaller child is `7`, so the result becomes `[1, 7, 3, 6, 8]` after the value moves down.

```java
class Solution {
    public void heapify(int[] nums, int ind, int val) {
        if (nums == null || ind < 0 || ind >= nums.length) {
            return;
        }

        nums[ind] = val;
        restore(nums, ind);
    }

    private void restore(int[] nums, int index) {
        if (index > 0) {
            int parent = (index - 1) / 2;
            if (nums[parent] > nums[index]) {
                swap(nums, parent, index);
                restore(nums, parent);
                return;
            }
        }

        int left = 2 * index + 1;
        int right = 2 * index + 2;
        int smallest = index;

        if (left < nums.length && nums[left] < nums[smallest]) {
            smallest = left;
        }
        if (right < nums.length && nums[right] < nums[smallest]) {
            smallest = right;
        }

        if (smallest != index) {
            swap(nums, index, smallest);
            restore(nums, smallest);
        }
    }

    private void swap(int[] nums, int first, int second) {
        int temp = nums[first];
        nums[first] = nums[second];
        nums[second] = temp;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)` in the worst case.

Space complexity: `O(log n)` because of the recursive calls.

This keeps the repair in one loop and does not compare the new value with the old one. At each step, it checks both possible problems: a smaller parent, or a smaller child. Only one direction can need work at a time in a valid heap.

When a child is chosen, it is always the smaller child. That prevents the moved value from staying above another child that is even smaller.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void heapify(vector<int> &nums, int ind, int val) {
        if (nums.empty() || ind < 0 || ind >= nums.size()) {
            return;
        }

        nums[ind] = val;
        int n = nums.size();

        while (true) {
            if (ind > 0) {
                int parent = (ind - 1) / 2;
                if (nums[ind] < nums[parent]) {
                    swap(nums[ind], nums[parent]);
                    ind = parent;
                    continue;
                }
            }

            int left = 2 * ind + 1;
            int right = left + 1;
            int child = ind;

            if (left < n && nums[left] < nums[child]) {
                child = left;
            }
            if (right < n && nums[right] < nums[child]) {
                child = right;
            }

            if (child == ind) {
                break;
            }

            swap(nums[ind], nums[child]);
            ind = child;
        }
    }
};
```

This also handles an update at the root, where there is no parent to inspect. A value at the last index has no children, so it can only move upward.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

